# Commit Result Notes Guideline

After completing any commit (especially when using in-editor git tools), produce a concise English note in chat summarizing the outcome so users have a clear record of what changed.

What to include (in order):
- Branch name and whether push to remote succeeded
- Commit message subject (first line) and a short bullet summary of key changes
- High-impact files or routes added/modified (only the most relevant)
- Any migrations/config changes and required follow-up actions (if any)
- Next suggested steps or verification checklist (1–3 bullets)

Formatting rules:
- Use short, skimmable bullets
- Prefer file references with backticks (e.g., `app/email-verify.tsx`)
- Avoid noisy details like full diff stats unless requested

Example template:
```
Commit complete and pushed to <branch>.

Subject: <first line of commit message>

Highlights:
- <concise bullet 1>
- <concise bullet 2>

Key files/routes:
- `<path/to/file>` — <short description>

Follow-ups:
- <verification or TODO 1>
- <verification or TODO 2>
```

Notes:
- If push failed, state it clearly and include the error summary with a recommended fix.
- If multiple commits were made in one operation, summarize the most recent commit and mention there were N additional commits.
